<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Celebration · c. 1328 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">In dulci jubilo</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Christmas carol from about 1328 that mixes German and Latin. A story says its writer heard angels sing it and danced with them.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>thought to be Heinrich Seuse; a further verse possibly by Martin Luther (1545)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous (melody first found in a manuscript of c. 1400)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>German Christians of the late Middle Ages; later Lutheran churches and English-speaking choirs</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Latin</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Germany</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>words c. 1328; tune in a manuscript of c. 1400; first printed 1533</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (medieval words in German and Latin, thought to be by Heinrich Seuse (c. 1328); tune first written down c. 1400)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/in-dulci-jubilo">In dulci jubilo</a>&quot; (&quot;In sweet rejoicing&quot;) is a Christmas carol whose original words alternate between medieval German and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, a mixed form called macaronic. They are thought to have been written around 1328 by the German mystic Heinrich Seuse. According to folklore, Seuse heard angels sing the words and joined them in a dance of worship; his biography, perhaps written by himself, tells how an angel came to &quot;the Servant&quot;, as it calls him, to bring him heavenly joys amid his sufferings, drew him into the dance, and began a joyous song about the infant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>.</p><p>The tune first appears in Codex 1305, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of about 1400 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> University Library, though it may be older. It was printed in Joseph Klug's Lutheran hymnal Geistliche Lieder in 1533 and in Michael Vehe's Gesangbuch of 1537. In 1545 a further verse, possibly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>, appeared in Valentin Babst's Geistliche Lieder, printed in Leipzig. The melody spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and appears in a Swedish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> version in Piae Cantiones, the Finnish songbook of 1582. Michael Praetorius set it many times, from 1597 to 1618–19, from simple settings for two to four voices to a festive version for up to five choirs with trumpets and timpani, and his settings were widely adapted in Protestant continental Europe.</p><p>Its fame in English rests on two versions. Robert Lucas Pearsall's translation of 1837 keeps the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> phrases and puts English in place of the German, and his arrangement for eight voices, adapted for four by William Joseph Westbrook and edited by Reginald Jacques in Carols for Choirs, is a mainstay of the Christmas service of Nine Lessons and Carols; a 2008 survey by BBC Music Magazine found it the second most popular choral carol among British cathedral organists and choirmasters. John Mason Neale's looser translation of 1853, &quot;Good Christian Men, Rejoice&quot;, has often been criticised: Thomas Helmore misread the old notation of Piae Cantiones, which produced its repeated words, and in 1921 H. J. Massé called the result a mutilation of a grand tune, preferring John Wedderburn's translation of 1567. The broadcaster Jeremy Summerly has argued that the mistaken repeated note is what makes that version memorable. Arthur T. Russell's 19th-century translation, &quot;Now Sing We, Now Rejoice&quot;, is sung in several Lutheran hymnals.</p><p>The tune has been set by many composers. Dieterich Buxtehude made a chorale cantata of it in 1683 and an organ chorale prelude around 1690. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> set it as a chorale, as a double canon in his Orgelbüchlein and as the chorale prelude BWV 729, written to accompany congregational singing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnstadt">Arnstadt</a>; another prelude long credited to him, BWV 751, has been attributed to Johann Michael Bach since the rediscovery of the Neumeister Collection in 1984. BWV 729 has been played as the first organ voluntary at the end of the Festival of Nine Lessons and Carols at King's College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, since the organ scholar Douglas Guest introduced it in 1938. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-liszt">Franz Liszt</a>, Gustav Holst and Norman Dello Joio used the carol too, Thomas Pynchon made it the choral centrepiece of an Advent scene in his novel Gravity's Rainbow (1973), and Mike Oldfield's instrumental arrangement of the Pearsall version reached number 4 in the UK singles chart in January 1976.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A Christmas song of joy at the birth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, moving between everyday German and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> of the church. It tells of heavenly joy at the coming of the infant Jesus. The mood is bright and dancing.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks sg-blocks-2"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">German</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">In dulci iubilo</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">nu singet vnd seid fro /</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">vnsers hertzen wunne /</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">leit in presepio /</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">vnd leuchtet als die Sonne /</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">matris in gremio /</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Alpha es et o /</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Alpha es et o.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">O Jhesu paruule /</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">nach dir ist mir so weh /</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Tröst mir mein gemüte /</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">o puer optime /</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">durch alle deine güte /</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">o princeps glorie /</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">trahe me post te /</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">trahe me post te.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Vbi sunt gaudia /</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">nirgend mehr denn da /</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">da die Engel singen /</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">noua cantica /</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">vnd die schellen klingen</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">in regis curia /</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Eya wer wir da /</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Eya wer wir da.</span></p></div></div><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Good Christian men, rejoice</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>With heart, and soul, and voice;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Give ye heed to what we say:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>News! News!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Jesus Christ is born to-day:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ox and ass before him bow,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>And He is in the manger now.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Christ is born to-day!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Christ is born to-day!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Good Christian men, rejoice</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>With heart, and soul, and voice;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Now ye hear of endless bliss;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Joy! Joy!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Jesus Christ was born for this!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>He hath oped the heav'nly door,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>And man is blessed evermore.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Christ was born for this!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Christ was born for this!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Good Christian men, rejoice</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>With heart, and soul, and voice;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Now ye need not fear the grave:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Peace Peace!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Jesus Christ was born to save!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Calls you one, and calls you all,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>To gain His everlasting hall:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Christ was born to save!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Christ was born to save!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Anonymous (14th century); as printed in Joseph Klug's Geistliche Lieder (Wittenberg, 1533), transcribed on German Wikisource. Text from <a href="https://de.wikisource.org/wiki/In_dulci_iubilo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>English: Free translation by John Mason Neale, in Carols for Christmas-tide. Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Carols_for_Christmas-tide%2FGood_Christian_men%2C_rejoice" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="MrweXsImVhg" data-title="In dulci jubilo: The Choir of King's College, Cambridge, in Pearsall's arrangement"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">The Choir of King's College, Cambridge, in Pearsall's arrangement</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel KingsCollegeChoir. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MrweXsImVhg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Musae Sionae and Polyhymnia Caduceatrix et Panegyrica (Michael Praetorius)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1597–1618/19</span><span class="sg-chain-h">settings for voices, and for choirs and instruments</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">English translation (John Wedderburn)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1567</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">In dulci jubilo, BuxWV 52 (Dieterich Buxtehude)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1683</span><span class="sg-chain-h">chorale cantata</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">In dulci jubilo, BuxWV 197 (Dieterich Buxtehude)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1690</span><span class="sg-chain-h">organ chorale prelude</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">In dulci jubilo, BWV 368, 608 and 729 (Johann Sebastian Bach)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">chorale and organ settings</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Gottes Sohn ist kommen, BWV 703, and Gott durch deine Güte, BWV 724 (Johann Sebastian Bach)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the opening phrase used as a fugal subject</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">In dulci jubilo (Robert Lucas Pearsall)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1837</span><span class="sg-chain-h">macaronic English translation and arrangement for eight voices</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Good Christian Men, Rejoice (John Mason Neale)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1853</span><span class="sg-chain-h">looser English translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Now Sing We, Now Rejoice (Arthur T. Russell)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">19th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">English translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Weihnachtsbaum, &quot;Die Hirten an der Krippe&quot; (Franz Liszt)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">piano setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Christmas Day (Gustav Holst)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1910</span><span class="sg-chain-h">choral fantasy using Neale's version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Variants on a Medieval Tune (Norman Dello Joio)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">variations for wind ensemble</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">In dulci jubilo (Ronald Corp)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1976</span><span class="sg-chain-h">setting for unaccompanied choir</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Gravity's Rainbow (Thomas Pynchon)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1973</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the carol sung in the novel's Advent episode</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">In Dulci Jubilo (Mike Oldfield)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1976</span><span class="sg-chain-h">instrumental arrangement of the Pearsall version, UK number 4 in January 1976</span></li></ul></section>
